  "notes": "Dutch-house and big-room, and the low-end architect behind much of the 2011-2015 EDM-pop boom. The entry names exactly what distinguishes his productions from the Swedish progressive-house school, and it is percussion: \"Dutch-house grammar — complex rhythms made from Latin-influenced drum kits — with tribal toms and congas layered over the four-square kick\". So this model carries a tom/tomLow/conga/timbale bed at 4-8 hits a bar at -2 dB, where Guetta, Garrix and Hardwell all run 0-2 riser lanes. The second Dutch-house convention is the low end: \"a lower emphasis on the bassline with the energy carried by the lead; where a bass is prominent it is a distorted sawtooth locked to the kick\", which is a kick mirror at 0.96 roots with a single passing tone — the least melodic bass in the producer block. And the third is the lead: \"a squeaky, high-pitched lead synth — a detuned saw or square pushed into the top octaves — as the drop's hook\", encoded as the highest melody register in this section, 79-103. Harmony is a two- or four-chord minor vamp because \"the Dutch-house idiom puts the identity in the lead timbre rather than the harmony\" `[LOW-CONF]`, a mark repeated here. ⚠ The research says the drum, lead and bass grammar come from the Dutch-house convention page; every velocity, density and probability above is INFERRED. ⚠ **`chords.harmonicRhythm` re-weighted (2026-08-17, harmony variety floor).** The entry's harmony line is \"minor-mode two- or four-chord vamps; the Dutch-house idiom puts the identity in the lead timbre rather than the harmony\", and the engine's `vamp` value is *one* chord held for the whole clip (`generators::chords`: \"One chord for the whole pattern\") — so weighting `vamp` at the top rendered a **one**-chord bed the research nowhere describes. The chord motion the entry states is now carried by `1_per_bar` (the pair over a 2-bar cell) and `2_bars_per_chord` (the pair over a 4-bar cell), with `vamp` kept as the minority for the held reading. A four-chord vamp needs four slots, which only `1_per_bar` gives. ⚠ The split between those two rates is **INFERRED** — the entry names the device, not its rate.",
